WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Multi-stage builds separate build-time tools from runtime artifacts.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:14.600
A build stage can use a .NET SDK image to restore packages, compile the application, run required build-time steps, and publish output.

3
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:21.580
A final runtime stage can then copy only the files needed to run the ASP.NET Core application.

4
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:28.340
This separation is valuable because build environments are usually larger and more privileged than runtime environments.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:37.800
They may contain package caches, source files, compiler tools, intermediate objects, test results, local configuration, and temporary files.

6
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.660
The final image should not inherit those contents by accident.

7
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:49.300
A clean runtime image starts with a runtime-appropriate base and copies published output from the build stage.

8
00:00:49.400 --> 00:01:00.760
The final stage should contain the application artifacts, required static assets, required native dependencies, configuration defaults that are safe to ship, and the runtime support needed to start the process.

9
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:08.460
It should not contain source code, test data, build caches, package caches, developer tools, or local settings.

10
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:14.580
Dockerfile instruction order affects caching, rebuild behavior, and accidental inclusion.

11
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.760
Copying project files before restore can improve cache behavior.

12
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:24.860
Copying the entire repository too early can make restore depend on unrelated files.

13
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:31.200
Broad copy patterns can also move more content into the build than the team expects.

14
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:37.080
Restore, build, publish, and runtime steps should be readable and reviewable.

15
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:50.300
A reviewer should be able to identify which stage restores packages, which stage publishes the app, what is copied into the final stage, which user runs the process, and which environment assumptions are set in the image.

16
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.060
The goal is not a clever Dockerfile.

17
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.160
The goal is a predictable artifact.

18
00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:01.100
Multi-stage builds make it easier to explain what the final image contains and why it contains it.
